WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:02.580
Instructor: In this section, we'll see another use case

00:02.580 --> 00:04.590
for the Git rebase command.

00:04.590 --> 00:07.320
It's one that I actually quite like using.

00:07.320 --> 00:09.570
It's called interactive rebasing,

00:09.570 --> 00:11.520
and we'll use it in this section

00:11.520 --> 00:14.010
to clean up our Git history,

00:14.010 --> 00:17.520
basically, to go back and reword commits,

00:17.520 --> 00:19.410
like not just the previous commit,

00:19.410 --> 00:21.510
but 10, 50, 100 commits,

00:21.510 --> 00:23.280
probably not that far back normally,

00:23.280 --> 00:27.000
but you can reword commits, you can edit commits.

00:27.000 --> 00:30.810
We can do something called fixing up and squashing commits.

00:30.810 --> 00:33.090
We can even drop commits entirely,

00:33.090 --> 00:34.410
and I'm gonna show you how to do all of that

00:34.410 --> 00:36.423
with the interactive rebase command.

00:37.260 --> 00:40.770
I will say that this is all gonna go in nice to have.

00:40.770 --> 00:43.710
It's, personally, I like using it.

00:43.710 --> 00:46.530
It's part of my workflow when I work with Git,

00:46.530 --> 00:48.630
but not everyone does this,

00:48.630 --> 00:51.660
and it's not something that really I could say is important,

00:51.660 --> 00:53.250
or critical, but I like it,

00:53.250 --> 00:54.630
and I think it's kind of fun, honestly.

00:54.630 --> 00:57.900
It's different then a lot of what we do with Git.

00:57.900 --> 00:59.790
Anyway, nice-to-haves.

00:59.790 --> 01:00.623
I wouldn't skip it.

01:00.623 --> 01:03.450
It's a short section, and I hope you enjoy it.

01:03.450 --> 01:05.490
It's kind of fun to go back, and edit,

01:05.490 --> 01:08.703
and manipulate Git history that's already happened, okay.
